[' Here is a high-level architecture for the requested event processing framework in Java. Note that this is not actual code but a design structure and components to be implemented.

```java
public class EventProcessingFramework {
    private Map<String, EventConsumer> consumers;
    private EventIngestionService ingestionService;
    private SystemMetrics metrics;

    public EventProcessingFramework() {
        this.consumers = new HashMap<>();
        this.ingestionService = new EventIngestionService();
        this.metrics = new SystemMetrics();
    }

    // Initialize components and set up integrations (APIs, data stores, machine learning)

    public void addConsumer(EventConsumer consumer) {
        consumers.put(consumer.getType(), consumer);
    }

    public void ingestEvent(Event event) {
        consumers.get(event.getSourceType()).processEvent(event);
        metrics.recordEventProcessed();
    }

    public EventSummary getProcessedEvents() {
        return new EventSummary(metrics.getProcessedEvents(), metrics.getFailedEvents());
    }

    public SystemMetrics getSystemMetrics() {
        return this.metrics;
    }
}
```

This design includes the core components of an event processing framework: an ingestion service, a system metrics object, and an interface for event consumers. The `EventIngestionService` class is responsible for receiving events from various sources, while the `EventConsumer` interface represents the different downstream services that process these events (e.g., data warehouses, dashboards, machine learning models).

The design provides a simple entry point to add new consumers and external integrations. The event object contains metadata, such as timestamps, source types, and event IDs. The system metrics object tracks important metrics like throughput, error rates, and resource utilization.

For the actual implementation, you would need to consider performance optimization strategies, fault-tolerance mechanisms (e.g., idempotent message processing, retry mechanisms), sharding techniques for scalability, and more depending on your specific requirements.']

