[' I will provide a high-level design for your modular Java application. This design uses a combination of Java standard library data structures, asynchronous processing, and a plugin architecture to meet the requirements:

1. Define an abstract base class `ApiService` with methods for fetching user data and clearing cache. Implement a thread-safe concurrent hash map (`ConcurrentHashMap<String, UserData>`) for caching fetched user data.

```java
public abstract class ApiService {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, UserData> cache;

    public ApiService() {
        this.cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    }

    // Fetch user data from API (abstract method)
    public abstract Future<UserData> fetchUserData(String userId);

    // Clear the cache for a given user id
    public void clearCache(String userId) {
        cache.remove(userId);
    }
}
```

2. Implement individual API service classes that extend `ApiService`, overriding the abstract method to fetch data from their respective APIs.

```java
public class ApiService1 extends ApiService {
    // ... implementation for API 1 ...
}

public class ApiService2 extends ApiService {
    // ... implementation for API 2 ...
}
```

3. Create a `PluginManager` to load and manage the API services:

```java
public interface Plugin<T> {
    Class<T> getPluginType();
    T createInstance();
}

public class PluginManager<T extends ApiService> {
    private final List<Plugin<T>> plugins;

    public void registerPlugin(Plugin<T> plugin) {
        plugins.add(plugin);
    }

    public T getService(String serviceId) {
        for (Plugin<T> plugin : plugins) {
            if (serviceId.equalsIgnoreCase(plugin.getPluginType().getSimpleName())) {
                return plugin.createInstance();
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No registered API service found with id: " + serviceId);
    }
}
```

4. Implement a `UserDataFetcher` class that handles fetching, caching, and retry logic using Java's `CompletableFuture` and an exponential backoff strategy.

```java
public class UserDataFetcher {
    private final PluginManager<ApiService> pluginManager;
    private final int maxRetries = 5;

    public UserDataFetcher(PluginManager<ApiService> pluginManager) {
        this.pluginManager = pluginManager;
    }

    // Fetches user data asynchronously with retry mechanism
    public CompletableFuture<UserData> fetchUserDataAsync(String userId) {
        CompletableFuture<UserData> future = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            ApiService apiService = pluginManager.getService("api-service-id"); // Get the API service instance
            return apiService.fetchUserData(userId).join(); // Fetch user data and wait for completion
        });

        return future.exceptionally(throwable -> {
            if (future.isDone() && future.get() instanceof UserData) {
                // Return cached data if it's already available or max retries have been reached
                return getCachedUserData(userId);
            }

            int retryCount = 1;
            do {
                Thread.sleep(Math.pow(2, retryCount) * 100L); // Exponential backoff
                future = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> apiService.fetchUserData(userId).join());
            } while (!future.isDone() && retryCount < maxRetries);

            if (future.isCompletedExceptionally()) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to fetch user data after retries", future.getException());
            }

            return future.join(); // Fetch user data and wait for completion again
        });
    }

    private UserData getCachedUserData(String userId) {
        // Return cached user data from the concurrent hash map
        return cache.getOrDefault(userId, null);
    }
}
```

5. Create a `UserDataController` that handles API calls and manages the `UserDataFetcher`.

```java
public class UserDataController {
    private final UserDataFetcher userDataFetcher;

    public UserDataController(UserDataFetcher userDataFetcher) {
        this.userDataFetcher = userDataFetcher;
    }

    // Fetches and returns user data synchronously
    public UserData fetchUserDataSync(String userId) throws ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
        return userDataFetcher.fetchUserDataAsync(userId).get();
    }

    // Fetches and caches user data asynchronously
    public void fetchAndCacheUserDataAsync(String userId) {
        userDataFetcher.fetchUserDataAsync(userId).whenComplete((userData, exception) -> {
            if (exception == null) {
                cacheUserData(userId, userData); // Cache fetched user data
            } else {
                clearCache(userId); // Clear the cache on error
            }
        });
    }

    private void cacheUserData(String userId, UserData userData) {
        // Cache fetched user data in the concurrent hash map
        cache.putIfAbsent(userId, userData);
    }

    private void clearCache(String userId) {
        // Clear the cache for a given user id
        cache.remove(userId);
    }
}
```

6. Implement the logging and monitoring features as needed, such as using `Logback`, `Micrometer`, or other Java libraries.

7. Create unit tests and integration tests for the different components of your application, including edge cases and performance bottlenecks.']

