Here are some code snippets for the Data Ingestion module (C++ using Boost.Asio for network I/O):

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <iostream>

namespace ai = boost::asio;

class DataIngestor {
public:
    DataIngestor(ai::io_service& ioService, const std::string& host, uint16_t port) : m_socket(ioService), m_endpoint(host, port) {}

    void start() {
        ai::ip::tcp::socket socket = m_socket.get_io_service().post([this](){ this->doConnect(); });
    }

private:
    void doConnect() {
        m_socket.async_connect(m_endpoint, [this](boost::system::error_code ec) {
            if (!ec) {
                receiveData();
            } else {
                std::cout << "Failed to connect: " << ec.message() << std::endl;
                this->doConnect(); // Retry connection after some delay
            }
        });
    }

    void receiveData() {
        auto buffer = std::make_shared<char[]>(1024);
        m_socket.async_receive(boost::asio::buffer(buffer.get()), [this](boost::system::error_code ec, std::size_t bytesReceived) {
            if (!ec) {
                // Forward data to the Data Processor module
                dataProcessor->processData(buffer);
                receiveData(); // Continue receiving data
            } else {
                m_socket.close();
            }
        });
    }

private:
    ai::ip::tcp::socket m_socket;
    ai::ip::tcp::endpoint m_endpoint;
    DataProcessor* dataProcessor; // Pointer to the Data Processor module
};
```

The above code is a simple DataIngestion class using Boost.Asio for handling network I/O. The actual processing of the received data would be done in the `DataProcessor` module.

For the rest of the components, you can choose different programming languages (e.g., C++, Python, or Go) and libraries based on your preferences and requirements. The modular design makes it easy to replace individual modules with alternative implementations or integrate third-party services as needed.

In terms of performance evaluation and system optimization, you may want to conduct benchmarks using tools like Google's PerfKit Benchmarker (gperftools) or Apache's JMH for Java projects. Ensure thorough testing strategies cover edge cases and failure scenarios to ensure the system remains robust and reliable under varying conditions.']

